WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:01.130
- I'm Ahmed Ali Akbar,

00:00:01.130 --> 00:00:03.400
and I host the See Something,
Say Something podcast

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:04.440
at Buzzfeed News.

00:00:04.750 --> 00:00:05.594
I'm here with my sister Zainab.

00:00:05.594 --> 00:00:06.427
- Hi!

00:00:06.427 --> 00:00:07.310
- [Ahmed] And we're gonna be making

00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:09.350
our mom's Eid lamb biryani.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:11.350
Islam is an Abrahamic religion,

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.151
and Eid Ul Adha celebrates
Abraham's sacrifice

00:00:14.151 --> 00:00:18.620
of the lamb and his son,
and so, during that holiday,

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:21.600
Muslims basically take the
meat of a butchered animal

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:23.580
and donate it to charity,

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.800
and share food with family and
friends and your neighbors.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:27.892
- [Zainab] The biryani, to me,

00:00:27.892 --> 00:00:30.510
feels like turkey on Thanksgiving.

00:00:30.520 --> 00:00:32.130
When we eat at our house on Thanksgiving,

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:35.050
we'd always have a turkey,
but also a biryani,

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.400
and like you were saying, every
family has their own recipe.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.030
There are people who have
like, their special tricks,

00:00:40.030 --> 00:00:41.790
and they swear like,
their version is like,

00:00:41.790 --> 00:00:42.750
the best version.

00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:45.330
And like, definitely,
people won't ever tell you

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.090
their full recipe, or like,

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:48.520
they'll leave out their secret ingredient

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.230
that makes it their special biryani.

00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:52.530
But, it comes out on special
occasions, like the turkey.

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:53.490
- [Ahmed] So, when I celebrate Eid,

00:00:53.490 --> 00:00:55.140
I usually send some money to like, a farm

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:58.490
to donate some meat to a family in need.

00:00:58.890 --> 00:00:59.800
It can take different forms.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.060
Some people do a cow,
some people do a goat.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:03.160
Some people do a lamb.

00:01:03.227 --> 00:01:07.069
But, the ultimate thing
is to share meat with

00:01:07.069 --> 00:01:09.940
people who need it, and the
people who are close to you.

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.920
- [Zainab] The tradition has
become to slaughter an animal

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:16.770
and to give the meat to friends
and family and to charity,

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.640
but the real kernel of truth
or importance to that vision

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.910
is to feed those in need.

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.230
So, usually, every year,

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.550
I donate to a place like
City Harvest or something

00:01:25.550 --> 00:01:27.873
so that hungry New Yorkers can eat.

00:01:27.873 --> 00:01:31.570
Eid Ul Adha not only celebrates
that Abrahamic story,

00:01:31.570 --> 00:01:33.850
but also celebrates the end of Haj.

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:36.120
It's on the 10th day of Dhul Hijja,

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:38.210
which is a month in the Islamic calendar,

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:40.760
and the people who are
doing the pilgrimage,

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.170
it's the last day of the pilgrimage,

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:43.120
and it's the day they celebrate

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.910
having completed the pilgrimage.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:46.140
- [Ahmed] It's a very happy holiday.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:47.880
People come together to share food,

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:49.800
celebrate all around the world.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:53.420
In Pakistani communities
in America and back home,

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:56.120
you're gonna be seeing
people eat a lot of biryani

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.510
and lamb and goat,

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.460
so when we celebrate Eid, we wake up

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:00.860
and go to the mosque in the morning,

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.850
meet our community, people
from all around the world,

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.160
and then we go to parties, usually,

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:08.230
where people are usually serving
meat to a bunch of guests.

00:02:08.710 --> 00:02:10.960
When we host a party,
we usually serve, like,

00:02:10.980 --> 00:02:12.550
three to five types of meat

00:02:12.570 --> 00:02:15.280
that are like, really complicated
and complex and delicious,

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.330
and biryani's usually the center of that.

00:02:17.510 --> 00:02:20.090
There's like, as many
biryanis as there are people

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:20.960
in India,
- Oh yeah.

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:21.793
- Pakistan, and Bangladesh--
- Yeah.

00:02:21.793 --> 00:02:23.320
- [Ahmed] And the sub-continent,

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.610
and there's so many ways to make it.

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:28.310
Our mother passed away about
a decade ago almost, now.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.500
She was a very good cook,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.280
and we have some of her
recipes in our head,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:35.590
but biryani was something
that was so special and rare

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.471
that I don't think I at least knew it,

00:02:37.471 --> 00:02:41.250
so I was really excited when
I found it in this recipe book

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:43.960
that her and a bunch of
other Pakistani doctors

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:44.810
had put together.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.930
Actually, the recipe we're
making is not exactly like

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:49.160
the one that I remember

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:50.960
because she never shared her secrets.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:51.850
- [Zainab] She never told anyone

00:02:51.850 --> 00:02:52.920
the full recipe,
(Ahmed laughs)

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:53.753
ever.

00:02:53.788 --> 00:02:56.790
- [Ahmed] Biryani is definitely
something that I associate

00:02:56.790 --> 00:02:57.623
as a party food.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.220
When guests are visiting our house,

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:00.440
that's when my mom would make it.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:01.640
- [Zainab] Yeah, it's a way to celebrate.

00:03:01.640 --> 00:03:05.000
We put so much energy and time
into all the different steps

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.200
and the layers of flavor, and that's like,

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:09.340
another way of showing your
family and your friends

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:11.490
how much you love them
and care about them.

00:03:12.412 --> 00:03:14.869
(upbeat music)

00:03:14.869 --> 00:03:15.702
- Mmm.

00:03:15.702 --> 00:03:17.687
- Oh my god.
- So good.

00:03:17.687 --> 00:03:19.770
♪ Oh yes ♪

00:03:20.201 --> 00:03:22.784
(upbeat music)

